data shell_typed_invocation_contract_note: String = "THE FIRST BOUND NODE, and it is bound to an ALREADY-GREEN validation on purpose. Until this row existed every node was ExecutionContractUnspecified, so dispatch_environment_admit refused on all of them and no part of the workflow below Env had ever executed end to end. A first binding therefore has two jobs that pull in opposite directions: prove the Verify path runs at all, and prove it discriminates. Those want different nodes, and conflating them is how a green pipeline gets mistaken for a working one. This row takes the FIRST job only — the validation it names passes today, so an Agent turn that changes nothing still produces a real admitted environment, a real derived command, and a real validation receipt. What that proves is the PLUMBING: capability admission, argv derivation, and receipt production, independent of whether the provider accomplished anything. It deliberately proves NOTHING about discrimination, and must not be read as evidence that Verify can fail — the discriminating red belongs on a second node whose validation is currently red and goes green when the work lands, and that node is the next binding, not this one.\n\nThe operation is the transport-clean corpus witness because it is what this ticket's own red control describes: 'a new unclassified production construction site reds the census'. It reads the live tree (SubstrateInputsOnly), so it is a statement about the repository rather than a fixture — a raw shell literal reappearing in that transport module reds it for real."

fn shell_typed_invocation_execution_contract() -> WorkItemExecutionContract {
  gunbc_claim_execution_contract(
    entry: "src/v2/test/claim/host_language_transport_script/corpus/migrated_transports_clean_test.dag" as FilePath,
    function: "corpus_dag_compile_clean_transport_clean_holds" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data observation_raw_print_retirement_contract_note: String = "THE DISCRIMINATING BINDING — the half shell_typed_invocation_contract_note deliberately does not supply. That one names an already-green validation and therefore proves only that the Verify path RUNS: admission, argv derivation, receipt production. It cannot distinguish a working verifier from one that returns green unconditionally, because nothing it runs is capable of returning anything else. A pipeline with only that binding is the coverage-by-illusion tier — machinery present, nothing gating on it.\n\nThis contract names a validation that FAILS today. observation_emit_frontier_count() is 8; the witness asserts 0, which is this node's handback stated exactly ('the classified zero-residue progress census'). So Verify has a target that can genuinely go red, the red is the ticket's own acceptance condition rather than a planted fixture, and the transition red -> green IS the work being finished. Together the two bindings pin both directions: one proves the path executes, the other proves it discriminates, and neither claim is available from the other.\n\nThe witness is enrolled QuarantineProbeExpectRed in gunbc.ci_layer_roots so a deliberately-red acceptance test does not red the corpus — the cadence built for precisely this, where greening is a counted un-quarantine event and not a silent pass."

fn observation_raw_print_retirement_execution_contract() -> WorkItemExecutionContract {
  gunbc_claim_execution_contract(
    entry: "dag/test/claim/observation_raw_print_retirement_acceptance_test.dag" as FilePath,
    function: "observation_emit_frontier_is_zero" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data roadmap_with_execution_note: String = "The per-node execution binding, shaped as a COMBINATOR rather than a parameter on every constructor (the `sign` twin below is the precedent, and it already carries `execution: rn.execution` through untouched). Threading an execution argument through active/active_small/active_large/authored/derivable/ticket_row would edit seven constructors and every one of their ~46 call sites to express a fact that today holds for exactly one node — the churn a combinator avoids, and the reason `sign` is a combinator too. A node without this wrapper keeps ExecutionContractUnspecified, which is the fail-closed refusal roadmap_execution_contract_frontier_note declares, not a default anyone chose."

data v1_lane_binding_survey_note: String = "A BINDING NAMES THE VALIDATION THAT CLOSES A NODE, so it can only be authored once that validation exists. A node without one stays ExecutionContractUnspecified, which is the honest fail-closed state rather than a gap: dispatch refuses with a typed diagnostic instead of admitting an environment for work whose completion nothing can check. Fabricating contracts to make a lane look dispatchable produces exactly the coverage-by-illusion tier — every node clickable, every Verify vacuous. The closing validation is written as part of the node's first slice; the contract follows the witness, never precedes it.\n\nTHE ROSTER IS NOT TRANSCRIBED HERE. This row previously carried a hand survey — 'two of six startable roots are bindable' plus the four names and why each lacked a validation — and it rotted exactly as a hand-maintained count must: two of the four it named (v1-test-hygiene-producer-retirement, pderive-typesafe-nullary-reflection) were accepted while the sentence still listed them as unbound, and the leading count was edited twice by unrelated changes. startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract() computes the same set from the nodes themselves, so read it there and delete nothing by hand. What stays here is the RULE, which does not change when the roster does.\n\nOne bound node was removed rather than added by this correction: see v1_test_migration_unbound_note. A contract whose validation cannot fail on zero progress is worse than no contract, because it reports done over undone work."

fn v2_emitter_producer_provenance_execution_contract() -> WorkItemExecutionContract {
  gunbc_claim_execution_contract(
    entry: "src/v2/test/claim/self_host/emitter_producer_provenance_witness_test.dag" as FilePath,
    function: "witness_restored_binding_without_executed_receipt_does_not_authorize_reds" as NonEmptyStr,
  )
}

data v2_emitter_producer_provenance_contract_note: String = "The validation is this node's red control stated almost verbatim. The ticket demands that 'a result recorded as made by the new generator, with no receipt from a run that actually produced it, has to be impossible to write', and witness_restored_binding_without_executed_receipt_does_not_authorize_reds asserts exactly that: a restored binding without an executed receipt does not authorize an emitter-produced claim. It is chosen over the suite's other twenty-six claims because it is the one that fails if the unrepresentability itself lapses, rather than one that checks a roster stayed in step."

data v1_test_migration_unbound_note: String = "v1-test-migration IS DELIBERATELY UNBOUND, and rebinding it to test_migration_delete_guard_holds would restore a false green. That binding existed and was removed here after reading what the guard actually computes.\n\nThe guard is DIFF-SCOPED. cli_run.rs test_migration_delete_guard_uncovered_deletes_inner resolves a (base, head) pair, returns an empty violation list when base_rev == head_rev, returns an empty list again when neither rev resolves and GUNBC_CI_DIFF_BASE is unset, and otherwise inspects only the v1 test paths DELETED between those revs. Violations are empty whenever a change deletes no uncovered legacy test file, so the guard answers true. Measured on the tree that carried it: the guard passes today while test_migration_debt_live_module_count() is 81, so a dispatched agent could do no migration work at all and Verify would still go green. A validation that cannot fail on zero progress cannot close a node (DESIGN 5 — a wrong answer must be loud, and 'done' asserted over undone work is exactly the silent wrong answer).\n\nThe guard is KEPT, unchanged, as what it actually is: a per-change deletion wall, wired through test_migration_delete_guard_holds in the debt witness module, refusing an unsafe deletion in the diff that attempts it. That role is correct and load-bearing. It simply does not authorize completion.\n\nThe node's own bar — 'the per-file coverage mapping with nothing unmatched' — needs a closing validation at the grain of legacy test BEHAVIOR, not modules: an admitted typed disposition for every discovered legacy test function, with unclassified as the derived set difference reaching zero. The module count is a trend metric that overstates debt by construction, because classification is exact-stem filename matching (chosen fail-closed after fuzzy matching wrongly reported an unrelated witness as covering `pipeline`), so a real witness under a different name still reads as debt. Until that behavioral disposition graph exists, this node is honestly ClosingContractMissing and appears in startable_nodes_missing_closing_contract, which is where a node whose completion nothing can check belongs."

fn with_execution(rn: RoadmapNode, e: WorkItemExecutionContract) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: rn.identity,
    node: rn.node,
    line: rn.line,
    carriers: rn.carriers,
    sizing: rn.sizing,
    execution: e,
    owner: rn.owner,
    signoff: rn.signoff,
  }
}

fn sign(rn: RoadmapNode, s: Signoff) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: rn.identity,
    node: rn.node,
    line: rn.line,
    carriers: rn.carriers,
    sizing: rn.sizing,
    execution: rn.execution,
    owner: rn.owner,
    signoff: s,
  }
}

fn carrier(path: String) -> List<Pointer> {
  [Pointer { label: "authority", path: path }]
}

fn fields(
  headline: String,
  boundary: String,
  displaced_cost: String,
  first_slice: String,
  red_control: String,
  out_of_scope: String,
  handback: String
) -> TicketFields {
  TicketFields {
    headline: headline,
    brief: boundary,
    displaced_cost: displaced_cost,
    current_state: concat("Unaccepted; next executable slice: ", first_slice),
    first_slice: first_slice,
    red_control: red_control,
    out_of_scope: out_of_scope,
    handback: handback,
    updates: [],
    authored_on: "",
    last_verified_on: "",
  }
}

fn active_sized(
  identity: String,
  id: String,
  owner: String,
  path: String,
  intricacy: Intricacy,
  volume: Volume,
  t: TicketFields
) -> RoadmapNode {
  RoadmapNode {
    identity: identity as RoadmapNodeIdentity,
    node: nid(s: id),
    line: TicketLine { done: false, ticket: t },
    carriers: carrier(path: path),
    sizing: Sized {
      intricacy: intricacy,
      volume: volume,
      repo: "gunbc",
      acceptance: ManualAcceptance,
    },
    execution: ExecutionContractUnspecified,
    owner: owner,
    signoff: Unsigned,
  }
}

fn active(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyMedium,
    volume: VolumeMedium,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn active_small(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyLow,
    volume: VolumeSmall,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn active_large(identity: String,
  id: String, owner: String, path: String, t: TicketFields) -> RoadmapNode {
  active_sized(
    identity: identity,
    id: id,
    owner: owner,
    path: path,
    intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
    volume: VolumeLarge,
    t: t,
  )
}

fn edge(child: String, parent: String) -> RoadmapEdge {
  RoadmapEdge { child: nid(s: child), parent: nid(s: parent) }
}

type SeedGrowthJustification {
  reason: String
  owning_dissolution_lane: RoadmapNodeId
  trigger: String
  current_boundary: String
}

fn observation_scoped_run_seed_growth_justification() -> SeedGrowthJustification {
  SeedGrowthJustification {
    reason: "Showing progress while a command runs has to reach the point where the old runner starts a program. It needs to know which piece of code was chosen, hold back error output until it can be shown in the right order, and report exactly one ending — and the rules for all of that live in the substrate, not in Rust.",
    owning_dissolution_lane: nid(s: "v1-hand-queue-drain"),
    trigger: "Move this reach-through into a described operation, then hollow out the corresponding part of the command-line tool so generated code does the work; the hand-written bridge is deleted at that point.",
    current_boundary: "dag/gunbc/observation_seed_render.dag; dag/test/claim/observation_seed_scoped_run_witness_test.dag; src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs; src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs; src/v1/stage0/tests/scoped_run_observation.rs",
  }
}

fn seed_growth_fields(j: SeedGrowthJustification) -> TicketFields {
  fields(
    headline: "Account for the five hand-written files that progress output still needs",
    boundary: concat(
      "Showing progress while a command runs still relies on hand-written code, in exactly these five files and no others: ",
      j.current_boundary,
    ),
    displaced_cost: concat("Why it is needed: ", j.reason),
    first_slice: concat(
      "Hand the job of deleting these files to ",
      concat(j.owning_dissolution_lane as String, ", without adding a sixth file."),
    ),
    red_control: concat(
      "Any new hand-written code for this outside those five files, or without this plan, is refused. The plan: ",
      j.trigger,
    ),
    out_of_scope: "Not a general list of hand-written files, not new progress features, and not a status report.",
    handback: concat(
      "Show that the five files are still the only ones, and carry this exact plan into ",
      j.owning_dissolution_lane as String,
    ),
  )
}

fn declared_roadmap_nodes() -> List<RoadmapNode> {
  [
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_44VKSHNX2ZNTDRCPGGNSFJWCWN",
      id: "2-scm-git-upstream-model",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Write down how Git really stores things",
        boundary: "Describe Git on its own terms: files, folders, commits, tags, file permissions, symlinks, links to other repositories, and how it names and finds them. Prove it by reading a small repository and writing it back unchanged.",
        displaced_cost: "If we invent a one-size-fits-all model first, it quietly drops things like executable permissions and symlinks — and every tool built on top inherits the loss.",
        first_slice: "Finish the storage description, then read one small repository and write it back unchanged.",
        red_control: "A plain file, an executable file, a symlink, a link to another repository, and a tag all stay distinguishable; writing against an out-of-date starting point refuses instead of overwriting; a missing object never reads as empty.",
        out_of_scope: "No shared source-control interface, no hosting or server work, and no guessing that a renamed file is the same file.",
        handback: "The model with citations to Git's own docs, the read/write-back receipts, the refusal receipt, and a list of anything we still cannot represent."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_5JX128MQ9XYR760B5CFC3VECTN",
      id: "2-scm-mercurial-upstream-model",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Write down how Mercurial really works, in its own words",
        boundary: "Describe Mercurial from its own documentation: its permanent change IDs versus the numbers local to one copy, its branches, bookmarks, tags, and its draft/published states. Use no Git vocabulary.",
        displaced_cost: "Mercurial's ideas are not Git's. Renaming them into Git words loses the differences, and a later shared design would be built on a mistranslation.",
        first_slice: "Write the description and read one small repository back out, leaving anything we cannot safely write marked unavailable.",
        red_control: "A copy-local revision number can change while the permanent change ID does not; draft, published, and hidden stay three different things; a bookmark, a named branch, and a branch tip cannot collapse into one idea.",
        out_of_scope: "No Mercurial support in the product, no shared source-control interface, and no faking missing facts from timestamps.",
        handback: "The description with citations, the read-back receipt, an explicit list of what we cannot do yet, and the differences that must survive into any later shared design."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_5MGS40RC9NE9HPFA4V5M2VVR19",
      id: "2-scm-pijul-upstream-model",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Write down how Pijul really works, in its own words",
        boundary: "Describe Pijul from its own documentation: individual changes that carry their own identity, what each change depends on, and how a channel is a set of changes rather than a pointer to the newest one.",
        displaced_cost: "Pijul does not have a 'latest commit'. Modelling it as if it does erases the thing that makes it different, and a third viewpoint is what stops us from mistaking Git's habits for universal truth.",
        first_slice: "Write the description, then apply a small set of changes, read them back, and try them in a different order.",
        red_control: "A channel cannot be reduced to one newest commit; one change keeps the same identity in two channels; applying a change whose prerequisites are missing refuses instead of applying an empty change.",
        out_of_scope: "No Pijul support in the product, no adopting its conflict rules as ours, and no shared source-control interface.",
        handback: "The description with citations, the round-trip and out-of-order receipts, and an explicit list of what we cannot do yet."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_629P2SF909R5C71V2HPCK4TJMD",
      id: "2-claim-indexed-evidence",
      owner: "deduction",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Say once what counts as evidence for a claim",
        boundary: "One shared way to record a fact, state a claim, and link evidence that supports or contradicts it — including how much a piece of evidence can prove on its own. Three existing features adopt it.",
        displaced_cost: "Right now each feature invents its own yes/no test, so a weak signal gets promoted into a confident claim. 'We stored a login' starts meaning 'the login works'.",
        first_slice: "Reuse what already exists for this, then run it through the three features that need it and check they agree.",
        red_control: "A stored login plus a failed live request supports 'we have a credential' but not 'the service is ready'; contradictory evidence stays contradictory and missing evidence stays missing, rather than the newest answer winning.",
        out_of_scope: "No source-control solver, no per-feature copy of the idea, and no treating 'we recorded this' as proof that it is true.",
        handback: "The shared model, receipts from all three features, the supports/contradicts/both/neither cases, and anything the substrate still cannot express."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(identity: "rnfx_DQV1HD42VR40BA8WBP1Y", id: "2-scm-compatibility-shape",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Extract the compatibility realization shape from three upstream receipts",
        boundary: "Consume the independently executing Git, Mercurial, and Pijul R0 boundaries and extract only their demonstrated roles: opaque target/generation handles, an independently required fidelity disposition for each retained or lost capture axis, projection/read-back identity, and modeled-or-unavailable conditional-advance capability. Native receipts retain every upstream-specific fact; dispatch is a generic handler value, never ScmKind or a vendor switch.",
        displaced_cost: "Lets the native edit → submit → outcome contract project through plural SCMs without forcing users into Git mechanics or erasing Mercurial/Pijul distinctions into a first-adapter abstraction.",
        first_slice: "Bind each R0 fixture through its native decoder and read-back assessment, replace C0's optional Git projection slot with realization-indexed receipts, and execute one fixed C0 proposal through Git, Mercurial, Pijul, and a synthetic fourth handler.",
        red_control: "C0 proposal identity, transition, and primary outcome stay unchanged across all realizations; missing or differently grounded conditional advance refuses; a read-back mismatch refuses; the fourth handler requires no primary-contract or kernel edit.",
        out_of_scope: "No semantic candidate space, admission policy, repair capability, executed transition, Applied receipt, universal commit/ref/channel type, hosted service, writable Mercurial/Pijul target, or central realization enum.",
        handback: "Return the shared role carrier, three native adapter receipts, fidelity/capability matrix, realization-indexed C0 projection with idempotent replacement identity, and the exact distinctions deliberately left native."
      )
    ),
    active_sized(
      identity: "rn_026DCFC306A7B44B061483AA71",
      id: "2-scm-p0-landing-spine",
      owner: "source-intent",
      path: "docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md",
      intricacy: IntricacyHigh,
      volume: VolumeMedium,
      t: fields(
        headline: "Make submit → landed the default source-control journey",
        boundary: "Model one submitted change through result finding, permission checks, committing, independent read-back, and compatibility projection. The normal user sees only landed, one concrete choice, or a plain reason the change could not land; synchronization and unfinished investigation stay inside the system.",
