        displaced_cost: "For text that is entirely plain ASCII this costs one allocation per character. For text containing any non-ASCII character at all, reading position N costs N steps and asking how long the string is re-counts the whole thing, on every pass — so the work grows with the square of the literal's length, and a single accented character anywhere flips the whole literal onto that path.",
        first_slice: "Move the one function onto the pre-decoded form its own file already builds, and replace its accumulator with a single buffer.",
        red_control: "Identical output over the corpus is the equivalence half and is satisfied by changing nothing, so it does not stand alone: a long literal mixing non-ASCII text with escapes must separate the two implementations by cost, and a deliberately wrong escape decode must change the output.",
        out_of_scope: "No general cursor or token-builder abstraction is introduced here. That generalization is worth what a second measured instance says it is worth, and the audit that would find one has not run.",
        handback: "The migrated function, the cost separation on the discriminating literal, and the corpus showing identical tokens."
      )
    ),
